# Where the API is, as a BROWSER sees it.
#
# `VITE_` is not a namespace, it is a promise: Vite inlines every variable
# under that prefix into the bundle it builds, and leaves everything else out.
# So this value is a property of the BUILD, not of the server that later serves
# it. Changing it means building again; there is nothing to restart. A `.env`
# here is therefore a development convenience, and a production deployment has
# to be built with the address it is going to be used against.
#
# It also means this is public. That is correct: the address of the API is not
# a credential. The credential is the session token, which is issued at login
# and never lives in a file.
#
# The address has to be reachable from the DEVICE that runs the JavaScript, not
# from the machine that ran `vite build`. A phone on the sofa cannot reach a
# loopback address on your laptop, however well the build went.
#
# No trailing slash. The client builds every request as `<this>/items`, so a
# slash here produces `//items` — which some servers route and some do not, and
# either way is a bug that only shows up against the one that does not.
#
# Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:3000, where `pnpm --filter @waymark/api dev`
# listens. That default is only ever right when the browser and the API are the
# same machine.
#
# Whatever origin ends up SERVING this bundle must be listed in the API's
# `WAYMARK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`. Otherwise every request is refused by CORS before
# the app is allowed to read the answer, and a browser reports that as a
# network failure — which sends you looking at the network rather than at the
# two lists of origins that disagree.
VITE_WAYMARK_API_URL="http://127.0.0.1:3000"
